\n\n. 2019 Dec 6:13:532.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fncel.2019.00532. eCollection 2019.\n\n# Forgotten Fibrocytes: A Neglected, Supporting Cell Type of the Cochlea With the Potential to be an Alternative Therapeutic Target in Hearing Loss\n\n[David N Furness](/?term=Furness+DN&cauthor_id=31866825)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Life Sciences, Keele University, Keele, United Kingdom.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 School of Life Sciences, Keele University, Keele, United Kingdom.\n\n-   PMID: **31866825**\n-   PMCID: [PMC6908467](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6908467/)\n-   DOI: [10.3389/fncel.2019.00532](https://doi.org/10.3389/fncel.2019.00532)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Forgotten Fibrocytes: A Neglected, Supporting Cell Type of the Cochlea With the Potential to be an Alternative Therapeutic Target in Hearing Loss\n\nDavid N Furness. Front Cell Neurosci. 2019.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nFront Cell Neurosci\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Front+Cell+Neurosci%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Front+Cell+Neurosci%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2019 Dec 6:13:532.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fncel.2019.00532. eCollection 2019.\n\n### Author\n\n[David N Furness](/?term=Furness+DN&cauthor_id=31866825)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Life Sciences, Keele University, Keele, United Kingdom.\")\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 School of Life Sciences, Keele University, Keele, United Kingdom.\n\n-   PMID: **31866825**\n-   PMCID: [PMC6908467](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6908467/)\n-   DOI: [10.3389/fncel.2019.00532](https://doi.org/10.3389/fncel.2019.00532)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nCochlear fibrocytes are a homeostatic supporting cell type embedded in the vascularized extracellular matrix of the spiral ligament, within the lateral wall. Here, they participate in the connective tissue syncytium that enables potassium recirculation into the *scala media* to take place and ensures development of the endolymphatic potential that helps drive current into hair cells during acoustic stimulation. They have also been implicated in inflammatory responses in the cochlea. Some fibrocytes interact closely with the capillaries of the vasculature in a way which suggests potential involvement, together with the *stria vascularis*, also in the blood-labyrinth barrier. Several lines of evidence suggests that pathology of the fibrocytes, along with other degenerative changes in this region, contribute to metabolic hearing loss (MHL) during aging that is becoming recognized as distinct from, and potentially a precursor for, sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL). This pathology may underlie a significant proportion of cases of presbycusis. Some evidence points also to an association between fibrocyte degeneration and Ménière's disease (MD). Fibrocytes are mesenchymal; this characteristic, and their location, make them amenable to potential cell therapy in the form of cell replacement or genetic modification to arrest the process of degeneration that leads to MHL. This review explores the properties and roles of this neglected cell type and suggests potential therapeutic approaches, such as cell transplantation or genetic engineering of fibrocytes, which could be used to prevent this form of presbycusis or provide a therapeutic avenue for MD.\n\n**Keywords:** Ménière’s disease; cell replacement therapy; cochlear fibrocyte; cochlear lateral wall; metabolic hearing loss.\n\nCopyright © 2019 Furness.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/f3ef5f7ff52f/fncel-13-00532-g0001.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/58560052d67a/fncel-13-00532-g0001.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nDiagrammatic representation of the cochlea…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nDiagrammatic representation of the cochlea in section (inset) showing the chambers of the…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nDiagrammatic representation of the cochlea in section (inset) showing the chambers of the spiral and the location of *scala media* (sm) *scala vestibuli* (sv) and *scala tympani* (st). The main panel shows a cross-section of the sm and the arrows indicate the two probable routes of recycling potassium through the organ of Corti, perilymph and lateral wall, including the fibrocytes.\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/321ca9ee1a1f/fncel-13-00532-g0002.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/0a2d6b763927/fncel-13-00532-g0002.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nLateral wall structure in a…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nLateral wall structure in a CD/1 mouse. The left panel shows a light…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nLateral wall structure in a CD/1 mouse. The left panel shows a light microscopy section of *stria vascularis* (sv) spiral prominence (sp) and the spiral ligament containing five types of fibrocyte (I—V). Scale bar = 50 μm. Panels **(A–E)** show fibrocyte types I to V by transmission electron microscopy. They have varying numbers of small processes (arrows) and differences in the density of cytoplasm (cy) and organelle content. Scale bars: **(A,C)** = 5 μm; **(B,D,E)** = 2 μm (adapted with permission from Mahendrasingam et al., 2011a).\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/264864ebae77/fncel-13-00532-g0003.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/91b1afd46e6a/fncel-13-00532-g0003.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nDiagram showing potassium and other…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nDiagram showing potassium and other ion circulation and the formation of the EP…\n\n**Figure 3**\n\nDiagram showing potassium and other ion circulation and the formation of the EP within the lateral wall. In the stria vascularis and the spiral ligament, K+ concentration gradients (\\[K+\\]) and potentials vary at different points in the tissue as shown. Gap junctions connect the spiral ligament fibrocytes together and also connect them to basal cells and then intermediate cells of the stria vascularis. K+ entering from outer sulcus cells of the organ of Corti or directly from perilymph *via* Na, K, ATPase or NKCC1 then flows through the syncytium of the ligament into the stria vascularis where it is transported to marginal cells and secreted into endolymph. For protein identity, see key below diagram. Modified figure based on Hibino and Kurachi (2006) with additional data from Adachi et al. (2013).\n\n [![Figure 4](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/eac74b89bcf8/fncel-13-00532-g0004.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/e503c5db757a/fncel-13-00532-g0004.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nSections from a 3 week…\n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nSections from a 3 week and 7 week CD/1 mouse cochlea showing accelerated…\n\n**Figure 4**\n\nSections from a 3 week and 7 week CD/1 mouse cochlea showing accelerated age-related hearing loss. Fibrocyte degeneration in these mice has been reported (Mahendrasingam et al., 2011b) and can be seen as loss of cell nuclei/thinning of the tissue (\\*). The evidence for progressive hydrops is indicated by the arrows, where Reissner’s membrane is distorted at 3 weeks, but this distortion becomes more exaggerated at 7 weeks, implying an increase in volume. Scale bar = 50 μm (adapted with permission from Mahendrasingam et al., 2011b).\n\n [![Figure 5](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/90b277b32e5f/fncel-13-00532-g0005.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7a49/6908467/28e3887c13fc/fncel-13-00532-g0005.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 5\n\n**\n\nMonolayer fibrocyte culture after immunofluorescence…\n\n**\n\nFigure 5\n\n**\n\nMonolayer fibrocyte culture after immunofluorescence labeling for caldesmon (green) and Na, K, ATPase…\n\n**Figure 5**\n\nMonolayer fibrocyte culture after immunofluorescence labeling for caldesmon (green) and Na, K, ATPase (red). The cells exhibit a mixed phenotype with some cells labeled strongly for both, and others labeled primarily for the Na, K, ATPase. Scale bar = 100 μm. Image taken from conference presentation, cited in published proceedings by Furness (2014).\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Adachi N., Yoshida T., Nin F., Ogata G., Yamaguchi S., Suzuki T., et al. (2013). The mechanism underlying maintenance of the endocochlear potential by the K+ transport system in fibrocytes of the inner ear. J. Physiol. 591, 4459–4472. 10.1113/jphysiol.2013.258046 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1113/jphysiol.2013.258046) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3784193/) - [PubMed](/23836687/)\n2.  1.  Adams J. C. (2009). Immunocytochemical traits of type IV fibrocytes and their possible relations to cochlear function and pathology. J. Assoc. Res. Otolaryngol. 10, 369–382. 10.1007/s10162-009-0165-z - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10162-009-0165-z) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3084383/) - [PubMed](/19277783/)\n3.  1.  Ahmed S., Vorasubin N., Lopez I. A., Hosokawa S., Ishiyama G., Ishiyama A. (2013). The expression of glutamate aspartate transporter (GLAST) within the human cochlea and its distribution in various patient populations. Brain Res. 1529, 134–142. 10.1016/j.brainres.2013.06.040 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.brainres.2013.06.040) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4420021/) - [PubMed](/23850643/)\n4.  1.  Alberio L., Locarno A., Saponaro A., Romano E., Bercier V., Albadri S., et al. (2018). A light-gated potassium channel for sustained neuronal inhibition. Nat. Methods 15, 969–976. 10.1038/s41592-018-0186-9 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-018-0186-9) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6834437/) - [PubMed](/30377377/)\n5.  1.  Carraro M., Park A. H., Harrison R. V. (2016). Partial corrosion casting to assess cochlear vasculature in mouse models of presbycusis and CMV infection. Hear. Res. 332, 95–103. 10.1016/j.heares.2015.11.010 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heares.2015.11.010) - [PubMed](/26707615/)\n\nShow all 69 references\n\n## LinkOut - more resources\n\n-   ### Full Text Sources\n    \n    -   [Europe PMC](https://europepmc.org/abstract/MED/31866825)\n    -   [Frontiers Media SA](https://doi.org/10.3389/fncel.2019.00532)\n    -   [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/pmid/31866825/)\n\n**Full text links** \\[x\\]\n\n [![Frontiers Media SA full text link](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/corehtml/query/egifs/https:--www.frontiersin.org-alerts-logo-PubMed.png) Frontiers Media SA](https://doi.org/10.3389/fncel.2019.00532 \"See full text options at Frontiers Media SA\") [Free PMC article](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/pmid/31866825/ \"Free full text at PubMed Central\")\n\n\\[x\\]\n\nCite\n\nCopy\n\n Download .nbib .nbib\n\nFormat: AMA APA MLA NLM\n\n**Send To**\n\n-   [Clipboard](#)\n-   [Email](https://account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?back_url=https%3A%2F%2Fpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2F31866825%2F%23open-email-panel)\n-   [Save](#)\n-   [My Bibliography](https://account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?back_url=https%3A%2F%2Fpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2F31866825%2F%23open-bibliography-panel)\n-   [Collections](https://account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?back_url=https%3A%2F%2Fpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2F31866825%2F%23open-collections-panel)\n-   [Citation Manager](#)\n\n\\[x\\]\n\nNCBI Literature Resources\n\n[MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/) [PMC](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/) [Bookshelf](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books) [Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\nThe PubMed wordmark and PubMed logo are registered trademarks of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). Unauthorized use of these marks is strictly prohibited.\n\nFollow NCBI\n\n[Twitter](https://twitte