n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2020 Oct 27:11:580639.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fneur.2020.580639. eCollection 2020.\n\n# On the Role of Fibrocytes and the Extracellular Matrix in the Physiology and Pathophysiology of the Spiral Ligament\n\n[Noa Peeleman](/?term=Peeleman+N&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Dorien Verdoodt](/?term=Verdoodt+D&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\")   [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Peter Ponsaerts](/?term=Ponsaerts+P&cauthor_id=33193034)  [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Vincent Van Rompaey](/?term=Van+Rompaey+V&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\")   [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Otorhinolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery, Antwerp University Hospital, Edegem, Belgium.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\n-   2 Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\n-   3 Department of Otorhinolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery, Antwerp University Hospital, Edegem, Belgium.\n\n-   PMID: **33193034**\n-   PMCID: [PMC7653186](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7653186/)\n-   DOI: [10.3389/fneur.2020.580639](https://doi.org/10.3389/fneur.2020.580639)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nReview\n\n# On the Role of Fibrocytes and the Extracellular Matrix in the Physiology and Pathophysiology of the Spiral Ligament\n\nNoa Peeleman et al. Front Neurol. 2020.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nFront Neurol\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Front+Neurol%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Front+Neurol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2020 Oct 27:11:580639.\n\ndoi: 10.3389/fneur.2020.580639. eCollection 2020.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Noa Peeleman](/?term=Peeleman+N&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Dorien Verdoodt](/?term=Verdoodt+D&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Peter Ponsaerts](/?term=Ponsaerts+P&cauthor_id=33193034)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\") , [Vincent Van Rompaey](/?term=Van+Rompaey+V&cauthor_id=33193034)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Otorhinolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery, Antwerp University Hospital, Edegem, Belgium.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Translational Neurosciences, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\n-   2 Laboratory of Experimental Hematology, Vaccine and Infectious Disease Institute (Vaxinfectio), University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium.\n-   3 Department of Otorhinolaryngology and Head and Neck Surgery, Antwerp University Hospital, Edegem, Belgium.\n\n-   PMID: **33193034**\n-   PMCID: [PMC7653186](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7653186/)\n-   DOI: [10.3389/fneur.2020.580639](https://doi.org/10.3389/fneur.2020.580639)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe spiral ligament in the cochlea has been suggested to play a significant role in the pathophysiology of different etiologies of strial hearing loss. Spiral ligament fibrocytes (SLFs), the main cell type in the lateral wall, are crucial in maintaining the endocochlear potential and regulating blood flow. SLF dysfunction can therefore cause cochlear dysfunction and thus hearing impairment. Recent studies have highlighted the role of SLFs in the immune response of the cochlea. In contrast to sensory cells in the inner ear, SLFs (more specifically type III fibrocytes) have also demonstrated the ability to regenerate after different types of trauma such as drug toxicity and noise. SLFs are responsible for producing proteins, such as collagen and cochlin, that create an adequate extracellular matrix to thrive in. Any dysfunction of SLFs or structural changes to the extracellular matrix can significantly impact hearing function. However, SLFs may prove useful in restoring hearing by their potential to regenerate cells in the spiral ligament.\n\n**Keywords:** cochlea; immune system; noise-induced hearing loss; sensorineural hearing loss; spiral ligament of the cochlea.\n\nCopyright © 2020 Peeleman, Verdoodt, Ponsaerts and Van Rompaey.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/31e4c7ac4efa/fneur-11-580639-g0001.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/3caeee33aa7d/fneur-11-580639-g0001.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nMid-modiolar section of a human…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nMid-modiolar section of a human cochlea showing the modiolus (MOD), containing spiral ganglion…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nMid-modiolar section of a human cochlea showing the modiolus (MOD), containing spiral ganglion neurons with axons to and from the auditory nerve (AN). The basilar membrane (\\*) and Reissner's membrane (•) define three fluid-filled spaces: scala tympani (ST), scala media (SM), and scala vestibuli (SV). The lateral wall (■) connects the basilar membrane to the otic capsule (7).\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/636be02e0ef9/fneur-11-580639-g0002.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/00f53cea32cd/fneur-11-580639-g0002.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nAnatomy of the spiral ligament.…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nAnatomy of the spiral ligament. (Left) Localization of the different fibrocytes subtypes in…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nAnatomy of the spiral ligament. (Left) Localization of the different fibrocytes subtypes in the spiral ligament. Cochlin is most abundantly expressed in the region of the type IV fibrocytes. (Right) Schematic representation of the different layers in the stria vascularis consisting of marginal cells, intermediate cells, and basal cells. Marginal and intermediate cells form cell processes. Circles represent blood vessels.\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/96594d1b92ab/fneur-11-580639-g0003.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/d35f/7653186/1ff444c58078/fneur-11-580639-g0003.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nRepresentation of the electrochemical milieu…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nRepresentation of the electrochemical milieu in the lateral wall (17) Top, scheme of…\n\n**Figure 3**\n\nRepresentation of the electrochemical milieu in the lateral wall (17) Top, scheme of the lateral wall and K+ transport apparatus involved in the generation of the endocochlear potential. The other panels show the predicted potential and K+ activity in each compartment under normal circumstances (second panel), during inhibition of Na,K/ATPase of SLFs (third panel) and during inhibition of Na,K/ATPase in marginal cells (fourth panel).\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Davis AC, Hoffman HJ. Hearing loss: rising prevalence and impact. Bull World Health Organ. (2019) 97:646–646A. 10.2471/BLT.19.224683 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.2471/blt.19.224683) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6796666/) - [PubMed](/31656325/)\n2.  1.  Schuknecht HF, Gacek MR. Cochlear pathology in presbycusis. Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol. (1993) 102:1–16. 10.1177/00034894931020S101 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1177/00034894931020s101) - [PubMed](/8420477/)\n3.  1.  Dubno JR, Eckert MA, Lee FS, Matthews LJ, Schmiedt RA. Classifying human audiometric phenotypes of age-related hearing loss from animal models. J Assoc Res Otolaryngol. (2013) 14:687–701. 10.1007/s10162-013-0396-x - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10162-013-0396-x) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3767874/) - [PubMed](/23740184/)\n4.  1.  Cunningham LL, Tucci DL. Hearing loss in adults. N Engl J Med. (2017) 377:2465–73. 10.1056/NEJMra1616601 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1056/nejmra1616601) - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6457651/) - [PubMed](/29262274/)\n5.  1.  Malgrange B, Belachew S, Thiry M, Nguyen L, Rogister B, Alvarez ML, et al. Proliferative generation of mammalian auditory hair cells in culture. Mech Dev. (2002) 112:79–88. 10.1016/S0925-4773(01)00642-6 - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/s0925-4773\\(01\\)00642-6) - [PubMed](/11850180/)\n\nShow all 93 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Review\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Review%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=